<p>The Pentagon said on Wednesday that Navy Secretary John Phelan was leaving his post &quot;effective immediately&quot;. Multiple outlets said the move followed months of tension with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth over shipbuilding priorities, with disagreements centered on the pace of naval production and the Navy&#x27;s broader fleet strategy. The change comes as the US and Iran remain locked in a naval confrontation around the Strait of Hormuz. Still, several reports said Phelan&#x27;s exit was not expected to directly affect current military operations because the Navy secretary&#x27;s job is mainly civilian and administrative, not command over deployed forces. Hung Cao, a Navy veteran backed by President Trump, is serving as acting secretary for now, giving the department an immediate leadership bridge as the debate over how best to expand the fleet continues.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/M3r-fe8KcBUJ76oW3J35YXPxxvjv_xMfwWnMmhamc4_c-mKfYMt4XwiQMmlkvxjGh1b-SunKmOYeSJmVx7Fyc2YDnUiXGyBWhijult2KBzt0tQ8H2xrkJ0w4DbqdavXJj0UGC5VpNwefs-qYS1feLhnMfXhhx1gGzulJYH61Hb6n5uu0xg-ew8rZMJZlkJYRAzMP34-Vi0LGEV0fLSOWJXsYQdO31F1RBnvEg4ST1mNq7tmrm491gqsBZ2dgXQ' alt='John Phelan during his tenure as Navy secretary before his immediate departure was announced.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Broader shake-up: Phelan&#x27;s removal followed the recent firing of the Army chief of staff and came during a wider stretch of Pentagon infighting involving Hegseth and other senior leaders.</li><li>Hormuz pressure: The leadership change coincided with a worsening maritime crisis after Iran&#x27;s Revolutionary Guard attacked three cargo ships in the Strait of Hormuz and seized two of them, according to The Week&#x27;s summary of the standoff.</li><li>Signal concerns: One lawmaker told Semafor the dismissal was &quot;troubling&quot; and said it sent the wrong signal to sailors, Marines, allies, and adversaries during wartime turbulence.</li></ul><blockquote>These were not U.S. s